# Forecasting, Cost, and Reporting

## Forecasting modes

Keep two explicit modes:

### Stocky Parity mode

Deterministic methods only:

- Last X days;
- custom date range;
- same period last year;
- fill shelves;
- fill shelves if below minimum;
- target stock level;
- Stocky ABC/U rules.

Parity calculations must be familiar, versioned, reproducible, and covered by fixtures.

### Smart Forecast mode

Optional capabilities may include seasonality, trend, intermittent demand, stockout adjustment, forecast confidence, rolling backtests, lead-time prediction, PO optimization, and anomaly detection.

Smart mode must never silently replace parity calculations. Label model, inputs, confidence, and explanation clearly.

## Formula discipline

Do not use LLMs for:

- demand arithmetic;
- Stocky-parity forecasts;
- ABC/U classification;
- reorder points;
- inventory quantities;
- average unit cost;
- landed-cost allocation;
- margins, markup, profit, or COGS arithmetic;
- forecast-accuracy metrics.

These calculations must use deterministic code or approved statistical models.

For every business formula:

- document inputs and data authority;
- define date inclusivity and timezone behavior;
- define returns, refunds, cancellations, stockouts, deleted variants, and missing data;
- define rounding and decimal precision;
- version the formula;
- expose an explanation trace;
- create boundary, tie, zero-data, and regression tests.

## ABC/U

Stocky Parity ABC/U must follow the approved product requirements, including the eight-week eligibility rule, deterministic ties, cumulative threshold behavior, and U classification.

Enhanced classifications must be separately labeled and must not overwrite parity ABC/U.

## Cost ledgers

- Use decimal-safe money values and explicit currencies.
- Preserve ordered cost, received cost, allocated landed cost, average cost, Shopify cost, and effective-date history separately.
- Do not overwrite historical cost evidence when a supplier price changes.
- Receipt reversals and corrections must create compensating events rather than destructive edits.
- Exchange rates and allocation methods must be frozen and auditable where used.

## Reporting

- Reports must identify their source facts and last refresh.
- Do not present stale or incomplete data as current.
- Totals must reconcile to drilldowns.
- Filters, exports, scheduled reports, and dashboards must use the same underlying calculation version.
- Preserve variant-level identity even when grouping by product, vendor, supplier, or location.
- Merchant exports must remain available according to approved retention and downgrade rules.

## Release evidence

Before calling forecasting, cost, or reporting work complete, provide:

- fixture-based parity comparison;
- calculation-version evidence;
- regression tests;
- reconciliation totals;
- performance results on representative catalog/order sizes;
- explanation output visible to merchants;
- documented remaining uncertainty.